Let me go through each cascade type the way I would explain it when a junior asks "why did saving the parent also save the children?"

`CascadeType.PERSIST` means when you call `em.persist(parent)` (or `save` a new parent), Hibernate also persists any NEW children reachable through the cascaded association. Without it, you would have to persist each child yourself.

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.PERSIST)
private List<Order> orders;
// em.persist(user) → INSERT user, then INSERT each new order
```

`CascadeType.MERGE` means when you `em.merge(detachedParent)`, Hibernate also merges the children, copying their detached state into managed instances. This is what you want when an entity graph was built outside a transaction (for example deserialized from JSON) and you reattach the whole thing.

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.MERGE)
private List<Order> orders;
// em.merge(user) → UPDATE user and its orders from the detached graph
```

`CascadeType.REMOVE` means when you `em.remove(parent)`, Hibernate also removes the children. This is correct for owned children — deleting a customer should delete their orders if orders belong only to that customer. The important limitation: REMOVE only fires when the PARENT itself is deleted; it does NOT fire when you merely remove a child from the parent's collection.

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE)
private List<Order> orders;
// em.remove(user) → DELETE orders, then DELETE user
```

`CascadeType.DETACH` means detaching the parent from the persistence context (`em.detach(parent)`) also detaches the children, so changes to them are no longer tracked.

`CascadeType.REFRESH` means `em.refresh(parent)` re-reads the parent AND its children from the database, discarding any un-flushed in-memory changes on the whole graph. Useful when you want to reset a graph to its database state.

`CascadeType.ALL` is simply all five combined. I use `ALL` for a true aggregate where the children's entire lifecycle is owned by the parent.

Now the crucial distinction between `CascadeType.REMOVE` and `orphanRemoval = true`. They sound similar but fire in different situations. REMOVE cascades a DELETE to children only when you delete the PARENT. orphanRemoval deletes a child the moment it is DISASSOCIATED from the parent — removed from the collection — even while the parent lives on.

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<Order> orders;

// REMOVE cascade: em.remove(user) → deletes user AND all orders
// orphanRemoval:  user.getOrders().remove(order) → deletes THAT order, user stays
```

Senior cautions. First, cascade follows the object graph, so `CascadeType.REMOVE` or `ALL` on a relationship to SHARED data is dangerous — deleting one parent could delete data another parent still needs. I cascade only to genuinely owned children. Second, `CascadeType.ALL` includes REMOVE, so applying it casually can cause surprise deletes; I am deliberate about it. Third, cascade is a JPA/ORM concern and is independent of database-level `ON DELETE CASCADE` foreign keys — they can coexist but are different mechanisms, and I keep them aligned to avoid confusion. Fourth, there is no cascade for plain queries (JPQL bulk `DELETE`) — cascade applies to EntityManager lifecycle operations, not bulk query operations, which bypass it.

